  "worked_solution": "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.02+0.24 \\\\ 0.18+0.56\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.26 \\\\ 0.74\\end{aligned}]\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
